From 0a68a948cab4e56896e433676728a481af7dfb90 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shikosu <teovidal01@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 14:27:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] AArch64: STXR with a value-tracking exclusive monitor, STLR
 (64-bit), ISB, and acquire/release barrier order

The exclusive store was approximate: the monitor held only the address, so an STLXR succeeded whatever
another thread had written in between, and two threads incrementing a counter lost updates. The monitor
now also keeps the value LDXR/LDAXR read (a second pseudo register, MONITOR_VALUE, next to MONITOR), and
STXR/STLXR store with __remill_compare_exchange_memory against it: the status is 1 when the location
changed, as the hardware's is. STXR (32/64-bit), STLR (64-bit) and ISB were not decoded.

Ordering, as the Arm ARM defines it:
  * LDAR/LDAXR: the barriers follow the load (they preceded it);
  * STLR: earlier loads and stores are ordered before the store, and a store->load barrier follows it
    (STLR and LDAR are sequentially consistent with each other); STLXR orders earlier accesses first;
  * DMB is a full barrier (store->store and store->load), not store->store alone, which a host that
    reorders a store with a later load (x86) needs;
  * ISB is a full barrier.
Byte, halfword and pair exclusives (LDXRB, STXRH, LDXP...) are still not decoded.

Checked against Unicorn's AArch64 core, and with four host threads adding to one counter in an
ldaxr/stlxr loop (no update lost).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
